.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd November 6, 2026
.Dt INTRO 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm intro
.Nd introduction to ABSD and its commands
.Sh DESCRIPTION
You are in a session on ABSD, a small x86-64 operating system.
The session service starts
.Xr absd-sh 1
for an authenticated SSH or enrolled local login.
For
.Ql ssh host command ,
it starts the native command directly or interprets shell syntax through
.Xr absd-sh 1 .
Two enrolled local text sessions are available; see local-console(7).
Files you read or write goes through one file server; see
.Xr filesystem 7 .
.Pp
ABSD is not a Unix.
Programs are started, never forked; a process holds handles with rights
instead of file descriptors and user IDs; there are no signals, no users,
and no permission bits.
The session is a single operator identity whose authority is fixed by
the session service when it starts; everything the session runs has that
authority or less.
See
.Xr process 7
and
.Xr handles 7 .
.Pp
SSH login shells start in writable
.Pa /home/operator .
Command requests start in
.Pa / ;
use an explicit
.Ic cd
when a scripted command needs a writable working directory.
.Pp
Start with
.Xr afterboot 8 .
For a downloaded preview, see its
.Dq Downloaded preview
subsection.
.Ss Login banner
An SSH shell with a terminal displays the static ABSD banner, the installed
release id, and
.Dq Authority is not ambient.
before its first prompt.
The release id is the image's compiled identity, checked against the file
server's selected release record before the SSH server listens; see
.Xr release 1 .
The art is included in the server at build time; no banner renderer runs
at login.
Command requests, including
.Ql ssh -tt host command ,
and shell sessions without a terminal print no banner.
No host name, memory, storage mode, or other status is inferred.
.Ss Commands under /bin
A bare command name runs
.Pa /bin/ Ns Ar name .
There is no
.Ev PATH .
Every shipped
.Pa /bin
program has its own section 1 or section 8 page.
Use
.Ql man name
to read one, or
.Ql man -k word
to search the installed descriptions.
The manual index follows the actual image composition, including
.Xr https-document 1
when that optional command is enabled.
.Pp
For file work start with
.Xr cat 1 ,
.Xr cp 1 ,
.Xr ls 1 ,
.Xr rmdir 1 ,
.Xr paste 1 ,
.Xr df 1
and
.Xr sync 1 .
Native commands describe the ABSD filesystem and streams directly.
.Pp
For current process state use
.Ql ps -A ;
plain
.Ql ps
inside the shell lists only that session.
.Xr interfaces 1
reports read-only interface observations, and
.Xr dmesg 1
reads retained kernel records from this boot.
These commands require an inherited read-only diagnostics grant;
they provide neither process control nor device authority.
For bounded retained service history, follow and export use
.Xr logs 1 .
Unmodified uutils 0.12.0 and ripgrep 15.2.0 ports retain upstream option
interfaces within the ABSD runtime's limits; consult their individual pages
for exercised forms and differences.
The C ports' pages describe supported options and explicit refusals; see
.Xr sbase 7 ,
.Xr toybox 7
and
.Xr tar 1 .
Programs are read whole through the file server at startup, so larger
ports start more slowly than small native commands.
.Pp
For the host C application interface and staged SDK route, see
.Xr porting 7 .
.Ss Deliberately absent
These are not missing by accident; each would need a mechanism or an
authority decision ABSD does not have:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Nm kill
and
.Nm top :
read-only system-wide process snapshots are available through
.Ql ps -A ,
but they grant no process-control authority. The bare
.Xr ps 1
builtin describes only the current session. CPU percentages are not reported.
.It
.Nm chmod ,
.Nm chown ,
.Nm touch ,
.Nm ln :
there are no owners, modes, or links, and nothing sets a file's time
(the one time a file records is described in
.Xr filesystem 7 ) .
.It
.Nm syslogd ,
.Nm cron ,
.Pa /etc/rc.d :
the boot starts a fixed set of services, including a dedicated bounded
service-log collector, not a syslog listener or general task scheduler;
see
.Xr logs 1 .
.It
.Nm mount :
a session's mounts are fixed by
.Nm sshd .
.It
Additional user identities and
.Nm su :
SSH public-key login and enrolled local-token login share one operator
identity; multiple sessions do not provide separate user accounts.
.It
A screen editor
.Pq Nm vi , Nm nano :
a program's keyboard is its standard input, which on a terminal session
the session line discipline edits a line at a time (no raw mode);
terminal size is observable with
.Xr terminal-size 1 ;
see
.Xr absd-sh 1 .
.It
.Nm fork ,
.Nm exec ,
signals, job control, process groups: see
.Xr process 7 .
.El
.Pp
There is no screen editor and no pager (above); the editor is the line
editor
.Xr ed 1 .
Write files with
.Nm ed ,
with
.Nm echo
and
.Ql > ,
or with
.Nm cp ;
read long output a part at a time with
.Nm head
and
.Nm tail ,
or pick lines out of it with
.Nm grep
or
.Nm rg .
.Ss Copying files
OpenSSH's
.Nm sftp
and default modern
.Nm scp
use the installed
.Xr absd-sftp 8
subsystem.
Run them on the client computer against the same reachable endpoint,
login key and host key pinning as SSH.
The subsystem starts in
.Pa /home/operator ;
see its manual for size and metadata limits.
A command and redirection remain available from the client:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ssh -p 2222 operator@127.0.0.1 'cat > /home/operator/f' < f
$ ssh -p 2222 operator@127.0.0.1 cat /home/operator/f > f
.Ed
.Pp
Both are byte-exact for any file (binary included), as long as no terminal
is requested (no
.Fl t ) .
See
.Xr afterboot 8 .
.Sh ENGINEERING SCOPE
The documented virtual operator profile is the ABSD kernel's
.Ql net fsd
durable boot under QEMU (x86-64, TCG,
.Ql -cpu max,vendor=GenuineIntel,+rdrand ,
one e1000 network card, one IDE disk image) running the file server
.Nm absd-fsd
and
.Nm sshd ,
reached from the host with OpenSSH.
The platform's
.Pa scripts/check-all.sh
exercises its named checks for that profile.
An engineering result is scoped to its exact source/image pair; these
pages do not establish qualification or physical hardware coverage.
The operator image is built and booted by the platform's
.Pa scripts/build-operator-image.sh
and
.Pa scripts/boot-operator.sh ;
see
.Xr afterboot 8 .
